Why Is High-Precision GNSS Becoming More Important?

Conventional GNSS receivers provide positioning for everyday navigation, but applications such as automated driving, surveying and machine guidance require higher accuracy, faster convergence and more consistent performance.

High-precision receivers can combine signals from multiple constellations and frequencies to reduce errors associated with the atmosphere, multipath and satellite geometry. Technologies such as Real-Time Kinematic (RTK) and Precise Point Positioning (PPP) use correction information to improve positioning performance, subject to signal conditions and service availability.

Multi-Frequency Receivers Strengthen Positioning Accuracy

Multi-frequency receivers are an important technology segment because measurements across several frequency bands help receivers correct ionospheric errors and improve ambiguity resolution. Single-frequency designs remain relevant where cost, power consumption and simpler integration take priority over the highest accuracy.

Chip suppliers compete on signal sensitivity, power consumption, interference mitigation, supported constellations, correction protocols and the ability to maintain performance in difficult environments.

Automotive and Autonomous Systems Drive Adoption

Automotive applications are a major demand center. High-precision positioning can support lane-level navigation, vehicle localization, advanced driver assistance and automated-driving functions when combined with other sensors and appropriate system safeguards.

Demand also extends to agriculture and construction equipment, where accurate positioning supports automated guidance and repeatable operations. Surveying, drones, marine systems and aviation create additional opportunities, although their accuracy, integrity and certification requirements differ.

Satellite Modernization and Positioning Resilience

Modern GNSS systems increasingly combine multi-constellation reception, correction services and sensor fusion. Integration with inertial measurement units, wheel-speed sensors, cameras and other data sources can help maintain localization when satellite reception deteriorates.

Jamming and spoofing are important design challenges. Manufacturers are placing greater emphasis on interference detection, signal authentication and reliable operation in demanding radio-frequency environments.

